# TODO - Build and distribution checks

## Objective

Verify build outputs, portability and distribution boundaries.

## Acceptance

- Keep third-party notices and required licence texts with distributions.
- Check startup, asset discovery, output size and target compatibility.
- Treat other desktop platforms as community-driven.

- Prove shared content is physically stored once across manifests.
- Reject unsafe or corrupted existing objects and mixed legacy stores.
- Test publication with tiny fixtures only; no multi-GB test exports.
- Verify configured package destinations and asset-root independence.

## Evidence

The native prototype package now includes the project licence and notices,
a target-filtered locked Cargo dependency inventory (34 packages, including
six local workspace crates), and 55 upstream licence or notice texts.
The inventory includes normal and build dependency edges but excludes
dev-only dependencies. The local package has no original game assets,
no bundled system libraries and one authored release executable.
The release hash and direct dynamic dependencies were checked and the
packaged executable passed a hidden native smoke with separate assets.
This is not a legal distribution audit or cross-host compatibility test.
Portable Linux, Android and Web builds remain unverified.

The package writer now shares the canonical bundle directory publication
primitive. A failed write removes its private staging on normal return
and does not publish a partial package. Tests exercise rollback,
non-component destinations and existing-output preservation. Abrupt
process termination and power-loss recovery have not been tested.

Native packaging now checks the ELF64 executable header and the complete
bounded program-header table before staging. Loadable segments must fit
the file, have valid memory extents and declared alignment, and contain
the entry point in file-backed executable bytes. Synthetic regressions
reject every truncated prefix of a minimal fixture, wrong targets,
missing tables, overflowing extents and non-executable or zero-filled
entry points. This is structural admission, not loader emulation or
cross-host ABI verification. Fresh validation passed in run
1795616397517689693-2582351. A current package attempt exposed a separate
licence inventory blocker for Ring; no new package was published.

The native licence inventory now admits the locked TLS dependency set.
Ring 0.17.14 requires its six reviewed licence documents, retaining the
relative paths for BoringSSL, ISC, once_cell and fiat-crypto material.
Synthetic checks reject each missing document and unreviewed versions.
ISC, BSD-3-Clause and the Rustls alternative licence expression are
admitted only with recognized licence documents. This is an inventory
check, not a legal clearance or system-library licence audit.

The current Linux x86-64 package succeeded: 48 declared dependencies,
88 files, a 3,224,528-byte executable and matching receipt SHA-256.
From an unrelated working directory, its explicit local launch catalogue
and touch smoke passed on the host OpenGL renderer with dummy audio.
The smoke submitted 600 movement frames and 51 combat frames without
missing its measured frame-submission budget. The dummy video backend
was rejected because it could not provide accelerated rendering.
Original media and assets remained outside the package. Physical input,
GPU presentation latency and cross-host compatibility remain unverified.

Authored native packaging now writes through the shared descriptor-relative
staging tree, checks each finished file's identity and metadata, verifies
the exact recursive entry set, synchronizes and rechecks before publication.
Only authored executables receive owner execute permission. Regressions
cover nested output, path collisions, substituted directories, changed
files and unlisted entries. The current package succeeded with 88 files,
a 3,224,528-byte executable and matching independent receipt SHA-256;
regular files had owner-only read/write permissions and the executable
had owner-only execute permission. Fresh validation passed in run
1795618311474658738-2984730. The staging parent and its ancestors must
remain trusted; this is not isolation against a malicious same-user
process or proof of power-loss recovery.

The native package receipt is now admitted through the same file and
aggregate byte budgets as every other package entry. Portable lexical
paths are checked before staging; missing or executable receipts are
rejected. Inline regressions cover the exact limits, receipt collisions,
overflow and nonportable paths.

