Configured package publication now rejects overlapping resolved asset and
binary paths before building. Two tiny fixture tests read a real private
.env, exercise a common deployment root, publish a package with separate
asset bytes, reject repeat publication and verify the asset bytes remain
unchanged. Unsupported target, absent bin parent and both containment
orders fail before publication. These are lexical checks; trusted local
ancestors remain required.

Configured packaging now also resolves existing directory aliases and
missing destination suffixes before comparing the two namespaces. The
same storage IR observation is used by configured migration. Tiny
fixtures cover both resolved containment orders, distinct destinations
under trusted aliases, repeat rejection, and absent or invalid
ancestors. Package admission does not create the asset directory.
Trusted, unchanged ancestors remain necessary for publication.

