A four-process visible X11 loopback diagnostic used the packaged binary
for both host and asset-free controllers. Three separately issued local
identities joined their assigned remote seats, moved and released, then
all responded after a 256-repeat synthetic input burst. Disconnecting
one controller while moving left the other two responsive; rejoining
with the same identity restored movement and release. Host frame
captures showed changes during movement and stable frames after release.
Only ephemeral private credentials and settings were used; private
keys were removed afterward. This establishes same-host package LAN
integration, not physical multi-host network or controller acceptance.

The native package receipt now identifies every included executable and
licence file by exact relative path, byte length and SHA-256. The
read-only package checker reuses the publication namespace and ELF
admission, bounds file counts and lengths, and rejects old receipt
formats, missing or additional files, links, case collisions, incorrect
owner-only file modes and changed content. Publication also performs
the readback. Tiny authored fixtures exercise intact packages, same-length
licence edits, missing nested documents, unlisted files and directories,
linked files, wrong access modes, malformed receipts and structurally
invalid executables with matching hashes.
The checker reports a receipt digest for independent comparison; it
does not authenticate a replaced receipt or establish legal clearance,
concurrent filesystem isolation, startup or cross-host compatibility.

The earlier configured-store cutover is now complete; see the native
